      "title": "Jobs",
      "description": "Jobs at Kingston University | Kingston University London Skip to main content Please enable JavaScript in your web browser to get the best experience. Please enable JavaScript in your web browser to get the best experience. Search Search term Search Course list Clearing Open days Future Skills Read the Future Skills report 2025 Kingston University is leading the way in the delivery of Future Skills in the United Kingdom. Our latest Future Skills report, launched at the House of Commons, is informed by extensive research. The report explores the skills most valued by employers in graduates, skills that are vital to drive innovation and boost economic growth. Clearing Study Experience Research Collaborate About us Search Menu Clearing Clearing Clearing Clearing Courses in Clearing Order a Clearing guide Study Study Study Study Search all courses Undergraduate Undergraduate Undergraduate Undergraduate courses How to apply Clearing 2026 Information for parents, carers and supporters Study abroad Join our undergraduate mailing list Postgraduate Postgraduate Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Professional placement Join our postgraduate mailing list Foundation Foundation Foundation Foundation courses Degree apprenticeships Degree apprenticeships Degree apprenticeships Degree apprenticeship courses Short courses Short courses Short courses Short courses Online courses International students International students International students International admissions timeline Visas, immigration and CAS Confirmation of Acceptance for Studies (CAS) Direct application for international students Country-specific information English language entry requirements English language programmes Study Abroad Programme Erasmus and European Exchange Pre-arrival information Arriving in the UK Fees and funding Fees and funding Fees and funding Understanding your fee status Undergraduate finance Postgraduate finance International student finance Welcome and Induction 2026 Welcome and Induction 2026 Welcome and Induction 2026 Enrolment Graduation Graduation Graduation Graduation ceremonies Certificates and transcripts Order a guide Order a guide Order a guide Undergraduate guide Postgraduate guide Experience Experience Experience Experience Commuting to Kingston University? Kingston Connect Adobe Creative Campus Outreach at Kingston University Student life Student life Student life Chat to our Student Ambassadors Achieving at Kingston University What our international students say Research student experiences Kingston Students' Union Mentee and mentor experiences Our campuses Our campuses Our campuses Penrhyn Road Kingston Hill Knights Park Roehampton Vale Visit the right campus Sports clubs, facilities and activities Accommodation Accommodation Accommodation Halls of residence Private student accommodation Equality, diversity and inclusion Equality, diversity and inclusion Equality, diversity and inclusion Inclusive curriculum Gender Equality Plan Athena Swan Diversity and inclusion calendar Elevate careers programme Transparency Women in STEM Careers and employability Student support Student support Student support Academic support Cost of living support Disability and mental health support Health and wellbeing IT support for students Head Start programme for undergraduates Estranged students Mature students Students who are parents Young adult carers Care leaver support Faith and spirituality Research Research Research Research Research degrees Knowledge Exchange and Research Institutes Knowledge Exchange and Research Institutes Knowledge Exchange and Research Institutes Behaviour, Business, and Policy KERI Cyber, Engineering and Digital Technologies KERI Design, Arts and Creative Practice KERI Health, Education and Society KERI Research facilities Case studies Collaborate Collaborate Collaborate Collaborate Services for business Services for business Services for business Knowledge Exchange Training and professional development Work with students and graduates Recruit Kingston University talent Intellectual property and commercialisation Degree Apprenticeships - Information for Employers Knowledge Transfer Partnerships Our partnerships Volunteer at Kingston University Facilities and venue hire Support us Support us Support us Why give? Legacy giving Community of donors Supporting student success through Future Skills Alumni Alumni Alumni Your alumni community Community engagement About us About us About us About us Contact us Future Skills Strategy and values Governance News Events Our academic faculties Our academic faculties Our academic faculties Kingston School of Art Business and Social Sciences Health, Science, Social Care and Education Engineering, Computing and the Environment Our staff Jobs Student login: Canvas My Kingston Read the Future Skills report 2025 Kingston University is leading the way in the delivery of Future Skills in the United Kingdom. Our latest Future Skills report, launched at the House of Commons, is informed by extensive research. The report explores the skills most valued by employers in graduates, skills that are vital to drive innovation and boost economic growth. Home About us Jobs at Kingston University Join a university where careers flourish. Explore roles that inspire and provide personal and professional growth in a dynamic academic environment. Page contents Show contents The application process Tips for our application portal Application and interview guidance Support for disabled job applicants Back to top Page contents Show contents You are reading: The application process The application process Tips for our application portal Application and interview guidance Support for disabled job applicants Back to top Jobs at Kingston University Advance your career at Kingston University Kingston University offers a variety of job opportunities across teaching, research and professional services. Explore our dynamic roles and find one that matches your skills and aspirations. Our commitment to equality and diversity creates a workplace where everyone can thrive, underpinned by industry connections and a vibrant academic community. In addition to a wide range of academic roles, our professional staff work in a variety of jobs, including Marketing, HR, Finance, IT, Estates and student support services. You’ll benefit from our excellent facilities, supportive team and proximity to London. Many roles include pathways for progression, learning opportunities and initiatives like flexible working to support work-life balance. If you're passionate and committed to making a real difference to people's lives, and delivering the best possible experience to students and staff alike, we'd like to hear from you. Explore more to find your perfect role at Kingston and see how we can help you achieve your career aspirations. Search for jobs Search for jobs The application process Submit an application online All current vacancies can be found on the search for jobs page . After browsing job details, you can use the 'apply' link at the bottom of the page to complete an application for a specific job. At this point, you will be prompted to save your details and set up a personal login account. During the application process, you can use the 'summary page' link at the top of the page, or the 'save' or 'cancel' links at the end of the page to return to the summary. If you wish, you can save and logout at any time. You can log in to progress and/or finish your application at any time prior to the submission deadline. Only when you submit your application will we proceed with advancing your interest in a vacancy. Reference details are part of the application. Upon receiving an offer of employment we will reach out to your nominated referees. Our online recruitment portal makes your application anonymous so that the panel members cannot see any of your personal details such as your name or address or any equalities data such as your gender. This is to ensure that shortlisting is as fair as possible. However if you upload a CV or publications list which includes any of these details then they will be visible to the panel. If you wish your application to be fully anonymous then please remove details such as your title, name and address from your uploaded documents. Attend an interview If you are shortlisted for an interview, we will email you a link to book your interview time online. Interview slots are available on a first-come, first-served basis. After booking your slot, a follow-up email will confirm the arrangements, including the format for your interview: Panel interviews: these are a panel of interviewers. This type of interview is the most common at the University. Panel members should normally be mixed in terms of gender and ethnicity. There will normally be a panel member who is external from the faculty or department. MS Teams interview: these may be used for initial interviews, or for candidates who cannot attend a face to face interview. If you are offered a MS Teams interview, you will be asked the same questions as you would at an on-site interview. Presentations: if a presentation is required as part of your interview, you will be notified of the topic and the duration. Tests: you may be asked to take a test or a series of tests as part of your interview process. You will be notified of this prior to attending. Tours: you may be given the opportunity to tour the workplace at your interview, to enable you to gain a better understanding of the working environment. Reserve candidates may be called to attend interviews at short notice. Receive an offer If successful at the interview the recruiting manager will contact you after the interview. We will normally make a verbal offer, followed by a formal written contract. The hiring manager will be in touch regarding arrangements of your first day, alongside alerts from the system with Induction and Welcome materials. Equality monitoring Both job applicants and current staff members can help the University to monitor equality in the workplace by providing us with information that will remain confidential. We use this information for monitoring purposes only; it is not revealed to your line manager or colleagues. This information can tell us if we are doing enough to attract candidates who have protected characteristics and can help determine how resources are allocated. Data Protection and Confidentiality Any information that candidates provide to Kingston University relating to the nine protected characteristics is classed as special category data under the terms of the General Data Protection Regulation. Special category data provided to or collected by Kingston University will be processed in accordance with the General Data Protection Regulation. We treat this data as confidential and will only be accessed by Kingston University staff with a legitimate reason for doing so in the course of their duties. Tips for our application portal As we manage all applications online, you may want to take note of the following advice: You can apply for multiple vacancies The standard information you enter such as contact details and education can be copied over to subsequent applications. Only job-specific responses, such as the supporting statement, will not be available. We strongly encourage you to ensure that your personal statement addresses the criteria for the position in which you are applying. Pages time out after 40 minutes If you do not save or move between screens the page will time out. Information already entered will be saved and you will be returned to the login screen. Request an alternative format for accessibility reasons Email HR@kingston.ac.uk for an application pack, or call the recruitment line on +44 (0)20 8417 3118, quoting the job reference number. Mobile access and preferred browser While you can submit an application via your mobile, we recommend using a PC for the best user experience. Microsoft Edge is the preferred browser to use for completing your application. Application and interview guidance for external candidates We want every candidate to feel confident and supported in demonstrating their skills, experience and potential. This guidance will help you prepare effectively for your application and interview at Kingston University. Application tips To give yourself the best chance of success: Read the Candidate Information Pack for a good understanding of what it is like to work at Kingston University and what our priorities are Read the job description and person specification carefully to ensure that your application clearly demonstrates how you meet the essential criteria Tailor your application to both our university and the role using relevant and specific examples Focus on your achievements and the impact you have made in previous roles to strengthen your application and show the value you can bring Ensure your application is clear, well-structured and free from errors Ensure your CV is up to date and tailored, creating a strong first impression, highlighting relevant skills, experience and achievements Interview preparation Before your interview, make sure you: Research Kingston University, our strategy, priorities and our values Review the job description and person specification in detail Prepare several strong examples from your experience using the SBO method Review your CV/application so you can talk confidently about your experience Prepare questions you would like to ask the interview panel Plan your journey and allow plenty of time to arrive Test your technology in advance if your interview is online Bring any documentation that has been requested Interview tips Questions you may be asked During your interview, you may be asked questions that explore how your behaviours and approach align to our values. For example: “Tell us about a time you worked in an inclusive way.” “Give an example of when you were innovative in solving a problem.” “Describe a situation where you showed ambition and achieved a challenging goal.” “Tell us about a time you took an enterprising approach to improve something or create an opportunity.” What we are looking for What you did and what your personal contribution was (I rather than we) How you approached the situation The impact of your actions How your behaviour reflects Kingston University values Other tips Use the SBO method to structure your answers: Situation – set the context Behaviour – explain what you did and how you approached it Outcome – explain the result and impact of your actions Be clear and structured in your responses Take your time – it is fine to pause and think before answering Be honest if you do not know something and explain how you would approach it Ask questions – interviews are a two-way process Our values At Kingston University, our values guide how we work and how we support our students, colleagues and communities. We look for candidates whose behaviours reflect these values. Ambitious We strive for excellence in everything we do and set high expectations for ourselves and each other. We are committed to continuous improvement and look for better, more innovative and enterprising ways of working. Inclusive We value and respect everyone for who they are and promote an environment free from discrimination. We all share responsibility for creating a culture where everyone feels included and respected. Innovative We look for better ways of doing things and encourage new ideas. We continuously challenge ourselves to improve and adapt, using available tools and opportunities to deliver positive change. Enterprising We actively seek opportunities and turn them into positive outcomes. We take initiative to create impact, drive growth and support continuous improvement. We are committed to an inclusive recruitment process. If you require reasonable adjustments throughout the application process at any stage please let us know. We have more information below on how we support disabled job applicants . Disability Confident employer As a Level Two Disability Confident employer, we are committed to guaranteeing interviews for all applicants with disabilities who have consented to being considered under this scheme and who meet the essential criteria from the person specification. As part of your application, you will be asked if you wish to be considered under the Disability Confidence Employer scheme in the declaration section. When there are occasions where we receive a high number of applicants for a specific role, we will select the disabled candidates who best meet the minimum criteria for the job rather than all of those that meet the minimum criteria. Supporting disabled job applicants At Kingston, we support external candidates with disabilities through the Recruitment and Selection Process. 1. Applying for a job at the University All of our roles are advertised electronically via our Recruitment system. This system has been classified as accessible, however when applying, if you require a job pack in an alternative format, you can contact the HR Shared Services team on 020 8417 3118, or HR@Kingston.ac.uk, quoting the job reference number. Depending on the requirements of our hiring managers, as an applicant you will be asked to complete an online application form, which will ask you a series of questions including the following: Personal Details (name, address, contact details) Employment History Education History Equality & Diversity Data – please note this information is not visible to our hiring managers. In addition to the above, you may be asked to complete a supporting statement and provide any supplementary documentation for your preferred role. 2. Equality information requested at application At application stage, you are asked to complete our Equality Monitoring Form (section 8b, ‘Telling us about your disability: equality monitoring’). This information tells us if we are doing enough to attract candidates who have protected characteristics as defined by the Equality Act 2010. We can take positive action to do so if we are not. The Equality monitoring information is anonymous and separate to your application and will not be seen by those involved in the short-listing process. 3. Requesting reasonable adjustments for interviews Kingston University is committed to ensuring our recruitment process is barrier-free and as inclusive as possible to everyone. We are committed to making adjustments for people who have a disability or long-term condition. We would welcome knowing about any adjustments you might need at the earliest possible stage so we can work with you to make sure we can accommodate any requests. Adjustments will be made on a case-by-case basis and designed to remove barriers or difficulties that you might face during the recruitment process. Some examples of reasonable adjustments could include: Providing the interview questions in advance of the interview Allowing extra time during the interview Holding the interview in an accessible room Holding the interview online over virtual meeting software if you cannot travel to the University Providing a British Sign Language interpreter or other communication support Providing the questions on paper or written in the meeting chatbox 4. Access around campus The University makes every effort to ensure that it is accessible at all times to staff, students and visitors. If you have concerns about accessibility, contact the HR Shared Services team on 020 8417 3118 or HR@Kingston.ac.uk , quoting the job reference number. AccessAble is an organisation that produces thorough online access guides for a huge range of venues across the UK. The AccessAble logo is available on many of the University’s campus and other web pages. Clicking on the logo will take you to detailed information about our campus buildings and facilities, such as the number of stairs, or where an accessible toilet can be found. 5. Access to Work interview support If you require a British Sign Language interpreter or a communicator for your interview, you will need to: Contact Access to Work , who will usually agree to provide funding over the phone. Contact HR. If you have a contact for an interpreter or communicator then please advise HR.  If you do not have a contact, HR can help you find a BSL interpreter or communicator to support you during the interview. Both BSL interpreters and Communicators will be fully funded by Access to Work and paid for, in the first instance, by the University’s Central Fund. The Equality Act 2010 defines protected characteristics as: Age, Disability, Gender reassignment, Marriage and Civil Partnership, Pregnancy and Maternity, Race, Religion or Belief, Sex and Sexual Orientation. 6. Post-offer work health assessment Following a successful job offer, all candidates are required to complete a work Health Assessment Form which will be returned directly to Occupational Health. Your completed form is not seen by anyone outside Occupational Health. If necessary, Occupational Health will seek your consent to obtain further relevant medical information. If adjustments are recommended or advised, they will seek your consent to inform HR, your line manager and anyone else who may be able to assist in providing those adjustments. 7. Applying to Access to Work on receipt of your start date If you require adjustments to your workstation or the way your work is organised and you have a start date, Occupational Health may advise you to contact Access to Work . Access to Work can appoint a specialist to carry out an assessment of your workplace and make recommendations based on the latest equipment and adaptations available. If an application is made within six weeks of your start date, approved funding required for reasonable adjustments will usually be paid in full by Access to Work. Once you have a start date, however far away it is, you can begin working with the University to make sure adjustments are in place for you when you start. Access to Work will ask for the name of your line manager which HR can provide for you if you do not already know it. The University is committed to ensuing all employees can thrive and achieve their best work. If you are, or think you might be disabled, or you know that you have a condition which will affect you at work, then the University will collaborate with you to make sure that appropriate reasonable adjustments are put in place and you have everything you need to thrive in your role. If when you were at school, college or university, arrangements were made to assist your learning or you were in receipt of Disabled Students Allowance, then it may be that similar arrangements will be required for you at work. Please note: Access to Work is a separate organisation and, while we will always support employees, the process is employee led. 8 Contact details Kingston University's HR department Human Resources (HR) department, Kingston University London Second Floor, Main Building Penrhyn Road Kingston upon Thames KT1 2EE HR Shared Services Team: 020 8417 3118 Text Relay: 18001 020 8417 3118 Email: HR@kingston.ac.uk Access to Work Jobcentre Plus Access to Work Operational Support Unit (OSU) Harrow Jobcentre Nine Elms Lane London SW95 9BH Email: atwosu.london@dwp.gov.uk Telephone: 0800 121 7479 Textphone: 0800 121 7579 Find out more about working at Kingston University Why work here? Discover why Kingston University London is a great place to work. Find out about staff benefits including discounts, memberships, parking and more. Learning and Teaching Enhancement Centre Find out about the Learning and Teaching Enhancement Centre at Kingston University London. Check out the range of support, programmes and frameworks available. Open Living wage logo in window Open Disability-Confident-Employer_Logo in window Open Athena Swan Gender Charter bronze award logo in window Open Race Equality Charter Bronze Award logo in window Open AdvanceHE logo in window Open Stonewall-Diversity-Champion_Logo in window Open Business Disability Forum logo in window Open British-Safety-Council_Logo in window Previous slide Next slide %%SLIDES%% Need help with your application? You should contact HR via email for assistance if you wish to: edit your application after submission or change/add supporting documents withdraw your application after submission report system issues and need support in applying for a post request an alternative interview date withdraw from an interview request feedback following an unsuccessful interview Contact us If you have questions or need additional support, feel free to reach out. Call us on +44 (0)20 8417 3118 or send us an email. Email the HR team Stay in touch Instagram LinkedIn Facebook X YouTube TikTok Threads Contact us Student policies Terms and conditions Accessibility statement Freedom of information Statement on modern slavery Privacy, data protection and cookies Kingston University, Holmwood House, Grove Crescent, Kingston upon Thames KT1 2EE Tel:   +44 (0)20 8417 9000 Copyright © Kingston University London, 2026. By clicking “Accept All Cookies”, you agree to the storing of cookies on your device to enhance site navigation, analyze site usage, and assist in our marketing efforts. Accept All Cookies Reject All Cookies Settings Privacy Preference Center When you visit any website, it may store or retrieve information on your browser, mostly in the form of cookies. This information might be about you, your preferences or your device and is mostly used to make the site work as you expect it to. The information does not usually directly identify you, but it can give you a more personalized web experience. Because we respect your right to privacy, you can choose not to allow some types of cookies. Click on the different category headings to find out more and change our default settings. However, blocking some types of cookies may impact your experience of the site and the services we are able to offer. More information Allow All Manage Consent Preferences Targeting Cookies Targeting Cookies These cookies may be set through our site by our advertising partners. They may be used by those companies to build a profile of your interests and show you relevant adverts on other sites. They do not store directly personal information, but are based on uniquely identifying your browser and internet device. If you do not allow these cookies, you will experience less targeted advertising. Performance Cookies Performance Cookies These cookies allow us to count visits and traffic sources so we can measure and improve the performance of our site. They help us to know which pages are the most and least popular and see how visitors move around the site. All information these cookies collect is aggregated and therefore anonymous. If you do not allow these cookies we will not know when you have visited our site, and will not be able to monitor its performance. Functional Cookies Functional Cookies These cookies enable the website to provide enhanced functionality and personalisation. They may be set by us or by third party providers whose services we have added to our pages. If you do not allow these cookies then some or all of these services may not function properly. Strictly Necessary Cookies Always Active These cookies are necessary for the website to function and cannot be switched off in our systems. They are usually only set in response to actions made by you which amount to a request for services, such as setting your privacy preferences, logging in or filling in forms. You can set your browser to block or alert you about these cookies, but some parts of the site will not then work. These cookies do not store any personally identifiable information. Back Button Cookie List Search Icon Filter Icon Clear checkbox label label Apply Cancel Consent Leg.Interest checkbox label label checkbox label label checkbox label label Reject All Confirm My Choices"
