    "https://jobs.kingston.ac.uk/vacancy/future-skills-research-fellow-quantitative-616618.html": {
      "title": "Future Skills Research Fellow",
      "description": "Future Skills Research Fellow - Quantitative (4913) - Kingston University London Search this site Kingston University London Search our site Share: Facebook Twitter Google+ Kingston University London Search our site Share: Facebook Twitter Google+ A–Z Advanced search Search this site Site search Facebook Twitter Instagram YouTube Pinterest LinkedIn Google+ Snapchat Courses News Events Faculties and schools Services for business Giving Jobs About us How to find us Contact us Home > Jobs > Search for jobs Facebook Twitter Google+ Jobs: Search for jobs Applicant advice Interviews and offer Joining us Joining us: About us Why work here Your career Frequently asked questions Future Skills Research Fellow - Quantitative Faculty/Directorate Faculty of Business and Social Sciences School/Function Faculty General (FBSS) Contract type Fixed Term Contract Location of vacancy Penrhyn Road Actual hours 37 Salary from 45,025.00 Salary to 48,820.00 Posted Date 30/07/2026 Closing Date 04/09/2026 Ref No 4913 Documents Candidate Pack Research (PDF , 10886.05kb) FBSS -8009721 - Grade 8 - Future Skills Research Fellow - Jul26 - Job Ref_4913 (PDF , 271.2kb) The Role Kingston University London is a national leader in Future Skills education, embedding the teaching and assessment of skills such as problem-solving, digital competency and collaboration into every undergraduate degree. This work has been made possible by funding from The Mohn Westlake Foundation (MWF) and is now entering a new phase of evidence-building and impact evaluation. To support this, we are recruiting a full-time Research Fellow, funded by MWF, to form the core delivery team for the next phase of Future Skills Research. As a Research Fellow you will bring strong quantitative, statistical and psychometric expertise to the project. You will be expected to: - Design, conduct and manage quantitative and psychometric research across the Future Skills Research Framework, including longitudinal tracking of undergraduate Future Skills participants and analysis of the Kingston Graduate Attributes Index - Analyse alumni survey, employer and engagement datasets to assess long-term skills use, career advancement and graduate-level employment outcomes across demographic groups, including disadvantaged and care-experienced graduates - Apply advanced statistical and psychometric methods (e.g. scale development and validation, multilevel and longitudinal modelling) to evaluate the impact of Future Skills on student and graduate outcomes - Contribute quantitative analysis to the mixed methods process evaluation of Future Skills implementation to feed directly into continuous curriculum improvement - Prepare and disseminate joint-authored research findings in high-quality peer-reviewed journals, conferences, policy briefs and white papers - Support the preparation of external research funding applications, contributing to the University's pipeline of income generation from. - Provide guidance on quantitative data collection, analysis and interpretation - Contribute to quarterly reporting to the Future Skills Research Steering Group and annual reporting to The Mohn Westlake Foundation - Participate in internal and external networks and public engagement activity (e.g. conferences, panel discussions) to exchange knowledge and build collaboration. Please note that this is one of two posts, advertised separately. The two posts are deliberately complementary: one bringing strong quantitative expertise, and one bringing strong applied, qualitative and pedagogic research expertise. Together they will deliver the analytical depth, methodological breadth and research capacity needed across the multi-year programme, while also building early-career researcher capacity and a pipeline towards external research income. This post will report directly to the KERI Director for Behaviour, Business and Policy (PI) who will allocate workload flexibly across work packages in collaboration with nominated Academic Leads. The Person Person specification – Essential - A PhD in psychology, social sciences, statistics or a related discipline, or equivalent research experience - Outstanding expertise in quantitative research design, including experience of longitudinal and/or psychometric research methods - Demonstrable ability to manage and analyse complex datasets using statistical software (e.g. SPSS, R, or equivalent) - Experience disseminating research findings through peer-reviewed publications and conference presentations - Experience contributing to research proposals and securing research funding, in accordance with quality standards - Ability to provide guidance to others on data collection, analysis and interpretation, and to help resolve methodological issues - Strong communication and IT skills, and the capacity to deliver outcomes on time and to agreed standards - Commitment to research integrity, inclusivity, and collaborative working across university departments and external partners. Person specification – Desirable - Experience researching student outcomes, graduate employability, skills development or education evaluation - Experience of psychometric scale development and validation - Knowledge of the UK higher education and employability policy agenda The Faculty This post sits within the Behaviour, Business and Policy (BBP) KERI, one of Kingston University's four Knowledge Exchange and Research Institutes, which brings together academia, industry and policymakers to generate evidence-based solutions to societal challenges at the intersection of human behaviour, business and policy. Both Research Fellow posts are essential to delivering Kingston University's Future Skills Research programme and offer a rare opportunity to help shape a flagship research programme from the ground up, working closely with researchers from across the University's four Kingston Engagement and Research Institutes (KERIs). Further information This is a full-time, fixed-term positions for four years , with a starting salary of £45,025 per annum . Please there is a possibility of extension to a fifth year depending on funding. Posts will be based at Kingston University, London Penrhyn Road campus Interviews are expected to be held on Monday, 21 September 2026. For informal enquiries, please contact Professor Debra Gray , KERI Director BPP. Bring your true self, expertise, and passion to Kingston where we are continuing to strive to build a university where authentic inclusion and belonging is at its heart. Every one of our staff and students’ identities, backgrounds and diverse experiences makes us who we are - so harness and embrace yours by applying today. We welcome applications from Black, Asian and Minority Ethnic backgrounds, LGBTQ+ communities and those with disabilities. We are a place-based organisation, proud of our location in Kingston and the civic role we play in our community. Our campuses inspire collaborative, innovative and creative communities. Being present on campus is important to us because it helps us to create a sense of belonging for both our staff and students. Therefore, staff are expected to be on campus for the majority of their working week. Candidates must be able to demonstrate their eligibility to work in the UK in accordance with the Immigration, Asylum and Nationality Act 2006. Where required, this may include entry clearance or continued leave to remain under the Points Based Immigration Scheme. To apply click on ‘Apply Now’. If, for accessibility reasons, you need to apply in an alternative format, please email jobs@kingston.ac.uk or call the HR Shared Services team on 020 8417 3118. Apply for this job Send to a Friend Contact us Human Resources Second Floor Main Building Kingston University Penrhyn Road Kingston upon Thames KT1 2EE Kingston University jobs: Tel: +44 (0)20 8417 3118 Email us KUSCO jobs: Tel: +44(0)20 8417 2138 Email KUSCO Jobs Search for jobs Current Vacancies Register Login Applicant advice Interviews and offer Joining us Contact us Human Resources Second Floor Main Building Kingston University Penrhyn Road Kingston upon Thames KT1 2EE Kingston University jobs: Tel: +44 (0)20 8417 3118 Email us KUSCO jobs: Tel: +44(0)20 8417 2138 Email KUSCO Jobs Search for jobs Current Vacancies Register Login Applicant advice Interviews and offer Joining us Contact us Human Resources Second Floor Main Building Kingston University Penrhyn Road Kingston upon Thames KT1 2EE Kingston University jobs: Tel: +44 (0)20 8417 3118 Email us KUSCO jobs: Tel: +44(0)20 8417 2138 Email KUSCO Jobs Search for jobs Applicant advice Interviews and offer Joining us About us Why work here Your career Frequently asked questions Site menu Home Undergraduate study Postgraduate study International students Research Frequently asked questions Site menu Home Undergraduate study Postgraduate study International students Research Alumni Courses News Jobs Search for jobs Applicant advice Interviews and offer Joining us About us Why work here Your career Frequently asked questions Site menu Home Undergraduate study Postgraduate study International students Research Alumni Courses News Events Faculties and schools Services for business Giving Jobs About us How to find us Contact us Disclaimer Copyright Website accessibility Website feedback Freedom of Information Wider Information Set Privacy Cookies Charitable status Facebook Twitter Instagram YouTube Pinterest LinkedIn Google+ Snapchat Jobs Search for jobs Current Vacancies Register Login Applicant advice Interviews and offer Joining us About us Why work here Your career Frequently asked questions Site menu Home Undergraduate study Postgraduate study International students Research Alumni Courses News Events Faculties and schools Services for business Giving Jobs About us How to find us Contact us By clicking “Accept All Cookies”, you agree to the storing of cookies on your device to enhance site navigation, analyze site usage